<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodelinda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 570–<a href="/years/628">628</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Theodolinda · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agilolfings">Agilolfings</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodelinda was a Bavarian princess who became queen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> by marrying King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/authari">Authari</a> in 589 and, after his death, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agilulf">Agilulf</a>. A Catholic who corresponded with Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a>, she encouraged the Lombards to move from Arian to Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. She founded the basilica at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a>, whose treasury keeps objects linked to her.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodelinda.jpg" alt="Theodelinda" width="320" height="528" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fratelli Zavattari · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodelinda_married_Agilulf_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Theodelinda was the daughter of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garibald-i-of-bavaria">Garibald I of Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waldrada">Waldrada</a>. Through her mother she was descended, according to tradition, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king Waco, whose family had ruled seven generations earlier. Her first husband was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/authari">Authari</a>, king of the Lombards and son of King Cleph, but he died in 590 after a brief marriage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a> may have had an interest in the match, since it joined a Catholic Bavarian with the Arian Lombards, as he had earlier promoted the marriage of the Frankish princess Bertha to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelberht-of-kent">Æthelberht of Kent</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-deacon">Paul the Deacon</a>, Theodelinda was so highly esteemed that after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/authari">Authari</a>'s death she was asked to remain in power and choose a successor. Roger Collins doubts this, and thinks political bargaining or force more likely lay behind her choice. Two months after Authari died she married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agilulf">Agilulf</a>. He remained an Arian but allowed their son to be baptized a Catholic, and his dukes worried that he had committed himself to the faith of the conquered. Agilulf did not let her faith shape his policy towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>.</p><p>Theodelinda corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a>, and some of the letters, which touched on her husband's conversion, are recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-deacon">Paul the Deacon</a>. She welcomed Catholic missionaries across her realm. Gregory sent her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> ampullae, a gospel casket and a gem-encrusted golden cross from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. Shortly before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agilulf">Agilulf</a>'s death in 616 he named her co-regent for their son Adaloald, and after Adaloald came of age she remained co-ruler. She was queen for some thirty-five years and her influence reached across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Theodelinda built a Catholic cathedral dedicated to St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and endowed it richly. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-deacon">Paul the Deacon</a> she also founded monasteries, among them one at Bobbio and later one at Pedona. The treasure of the cathedral at Monza includes a gilded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> sculpture of a hen with her chicks, which was probably another gift from Gregory. Her work helped restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a> to primacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> against its rival, Arian Christianity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theodelinda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the start the duchy stood under Frankish overlordship, yet the Agilolfings had wide connections: Garibald's daughter <b>Theodelinda</b> married the Lombard king Authari in 589 and later Agilulf, and became a leading figure in Lombard Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Authari, king of the Lombards, married <b>Theodelinda</b>, daughter of the Bavarian duke Garibald I, at Verona, in a political alliance meant to reinforce his royal position.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-15">15 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Queen <b>Theodelinda</b> the Lombards began to move from Arian to Catholic Christianity.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Kingdom of the Lombards</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Regensburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.02,12.08&amp;year=607" aria-label="Regensburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4829 453 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Theodelinda"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5279 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5279 723h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.02,12.08&amp;year=607">What was here in 607 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
